KZ2 patch 6027: lv2 mutex semantics (guest-thread ownership, hand-off at unlock and cond signal, lv2 destroy rules).

After R&C patch 7007 (Ratchet & Clank PC port, same owner, MIT), ported onto KZ2's tree with
patch 6025's diagnostics kept. Behaviour as RPCS3's lv2_mutex / lv2_cond (read, not copied).
The mutex was a host CRITICAL_SECTION (pthread mutex) per slot, owned by a HOST thread, beside bookkeeping
(owner_tid, lock_count) keyed by the GUEST thread id; sys_cond_wait re-locked it itself after the wake and destroy
deleted it whatever its state. Now the mutex is the bookkeeping: owner / count / FIFO of waiters under a short host
lock. unlock hands it to the first queued locker; a cond signal hands it to the woken waiter (or queues the waiter on
the mutex when it is owned); a timed-out cond_wait queues like a locker and returns ETIMEDOUT once it owns the mutex
again; destroy is ESRCH, then EBUSY while owned, then EPERM while a cond is bound (cond create/destroy count the
binding); create checks its attribute (EFAULT, EINVAL); trylock by the owner of a non-recursive mutex is EDEADLK;
sys_cond_signal_to wakes only the named thread (EPERM if it does not wait); slots are allocated next-fit and their
host locks are never destroyed.
Killzone 2 (the RE notes earlier change): not the cause of the intro-skip pause (that was the shared guest-call stack,
patch 6028; with this patch alone the pause still came in 3 of 6 runs); kept as lv2 semantics, host-tested.
Patch 6025's diagnostics stay and grow: the ring keeps 16 operations with the host thread and owner.count after each
(ops L T t E X U u W R C D d), and a timed-out lock or a thread exiting with a mutex prints the outer locker of the
current ownership with its guest call chain ("owned since").
ASSUMPTION: FIFO hand-off for every protocol (lv2 orders SYS_SYNC_PRIORITY waiters by thread priority).
Host test: tools/runtime/test_lv2_mutex.sh. Upstream candidate (with R&C).

Rebased on ps3recomp v0.18.0 (hand-merged): sys_cond.c and sys_mutex.h taken as the patch side whole (upstream #474 is POSIX-only; #490's destroy rule is covered by 6027); the opt-in PS3_PRIO_START note_block in sys_mutex is not carried

